{ id:'enthd-ear-122', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'In children with **mastoid abscess**, the post-auricular incision must be **……. in order to prevent facial nerve injury**?',
  options:['High oblique','High transverse','Low oblique','Low transverse'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**High — because the infant has no mastoid process, so the facial nerve leaves the stylomastoid foramen almost directly under the post-auricular skin, and a low incision cuts it.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` states the rule itself, under iatrogenic causes of facial paralysis: *"Iatrogenic (Unintentional surgical injury): a- **Incision of a postauricular abscess in infant. Incision must be placed high.** b- Classical cortical & radical mastoidectomies. c- Congenital ear surgery. d- Surgery of CPA."* The same deck traces the nerve to *"the **Mastoid (vertical) segment** which ends by the **stylomastoid foramen**"*.\n\n**Why height is the variable that matters** *(the developmental reason is not stated on the slide and is not taken from the course material)*: the mastoid process is not present at birth. It develops through the first years as the mastoid pneumatises and the sternomastoid pulls on it. Until then the stylomastoid foramen sits **superficially and high**, unprotected by any bony prominence, so the emerging trunk lies within a few millimetres of the skin at the lower end of the post-auricular sulcus. **Cut low and the knife is in the nerve.**\n\n**Where the abscess comes from.** This is the classical post-auricular abscess of acute mastoiditis — the outer cortex erodes, pus collects under the periosteum, and `L16) Chronic otitis media.pdf` describes the resulting fluctuant post-auricular swelling with the auricle pushed **outwards and downwards**; the same deck notes acute mastoiditis is *"more common in children"*.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **High oblique** | **keyed** — high clears the nerve, and the incision is angled along the post-auricular sulcus so that it parallels the skin creases and gives access to the cortex |\n| **High transverse** | the height is right, so this is the near miss. It is rejected on the **direction** only, which is the weaker half of the question |\n| **Low oblique** | correct direction, wrong height — and height is the whole point of the slide’s instruction |\n| **Low transverse** | wrong on both counts, and the classic way to produce an iatrogenic facial palsy in an infant |\n\n**⚠️ The word "oblique" is not on any cached ENT slide.** A grep of all 34 cached decks returns *oblique* only in laryngeal and pharyngeal anatomy. The deck teaches **high**, not **high oblique**, so the choice between A and B is decided outside the course material: the standard post-auricular incision follows the curve of the sulcus about a finger’s breadth behind it, i.e. **obliquely**, not as a straight horizontal cut *(not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Bottom line: in a child, go HIGH. The nerve is where the mastoid process is not yet.** Grade Gain asks the same fact as a "why" question and keys *"To avoid damage to the facial nerve and prevent facial palsy"* — `entqb-ear5-149`, the two banks in agreement.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'An infant has no mastoid process, so the facial nerve emerges superficially behind the ear — a post-auricular incision for a mastoid abscess must be placed high, and it follows the sulcus obliquely.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.50 (Ear ch.6, Facial nerve). Secondary chapter: `ent-csom`, where acute mastoiditis and its post-auricular abscess are taught. *"Incision of a postauricular abscess in infant. Incision must be placed high."* and the mastoid segment ending at the stylomastoid foramen are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`; the post-auricular swelling of acute mastoiditis from `L16) Chronic otitis media.pdf`. **⚠️ The deck teaches HIGH but never writes "oblique" — the A-versus-B discrimination is supplied from general knowledge and tagged in the explanation.** Cross-bank agreement recorded with `entqb-ear5-149`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-123', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'Which of the following tests — that assess **facial nerve lesions** — is a **quantitative** test?',
  options:['Maximum nerve excitability test','Electroneuronography','Electromyography','B&C together'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Electroneuronography — the lecture says so in those exact words.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`, under *Degree of the lesion*: *"2- **Electroneuronography (ENOG) = Evoked electromyography**: Similar to maximum stimulation test in which the evoked CAP are recorded. **The height of the action potential is compared with the good side and a percentage comparison is made. It is a quantitative test.** Degeneration of more than 90% means recovery is poor."*\n\n**Why it is the quantitative one.** ENoG stimulates the nerve maximally and records the compound action potential from the muscle on each side, then expresses the paralysed side as a **percentage** of the healthy side. That percentage is a number, it can be tracked serially, and it carries a decision threshold — **90 % degeneration**, above which the deck sends the patient for decompression: *"Surgical treatment: For complete paralysis with more than 90% degeneration by ENOG within 6 days."*\n\n| Option | What it actually yields |\n|---|---|\n| **Maximum nerve excitability test (Hilger)** | a **comparison**, not a measurement — *"The response on the involved side is compared to the normal side"*. It tells you whether the distal segment still conducts, and it is *"ideally started on the third post onset day"* because a cut nerve keeps conducting for **24–72 hours** before Wallerian degeneration silences it. Qualitative |\n| **Electroneuronography** | **keyed** — a percentage |\n| **Electromyography** | needle recording of what the muscle is doing: *"the presence of voluntary CAP indicates less than total transaction"* in the first 10 days, *"the presence of **fibrillation** indicates denervation"* after 10–14 days. Those are **present/absent** findings — qualitative, and prognostic only after 10 weeks |\n| **B&C together** | fails because EMG is not quantitative; that combined option is the key of the **next** question, which asks about **prognosis** instead |\n\n**⚠️ Read the adjective in the stem.** This question and `enthd-ear-124` print the identical four options and differ by one word — *quantitative* here, *prognostic* there — and the keys are different. **Quantitative → ENoG alone. Prognostic → ENoG and EMG together.**\n\nCross-references: `entqb-ear5-137` (when the Hilger test is ideally done), `entqb-ear5-138` (when EMG becomes prognostic), `entqb-ear5-142` (what >90 % degeneration on ENoG means).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'ENoG is the quantitative electrical test of the facial nerve — it expresses the evoked potential of the paralysed side as a percentage of the normal side, with 90 % degeneration as the surgical threshold.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.50 (Ear ch.6). *"It is a quantitative test"*, the Hilger comparison with its 24–72 hour window and third-day timing, the EMG findings and the 90 %/6-day surgical rule are all transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`. **Deliberately paired with `enthd-ear-124`, printed immediately below with the same four options and a different adjective.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-124', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'Which of the following tests — that assess **facial nerve lesions** — is a **prognostic** test?',
  options:['Maximum nerve excitability test','Electroneuronography','Electromyography','B&C together'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**B and C together — the deck attaches a prognostic statement to ENoG and to EMG, and to neither of the others.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` gives each test its own line, and two of the three end in a prediction:\n\n| Test | The deck’s own words | Prognostic? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Hilger / maximum nerve excitability** | *"The response on the involved side is compared to the normal side… Ideally testing is started on the third post onset day"* | **no** — it establishes whether the distal segment still conducts, nothing more |\n| **ENoG** | *"**Degeneration of more than 90% means recovery is poor**"* | **yes** — and the same threshold decides surgery: *"complete paralysis with more than 90% degeneration by ENOG within 6 days: Decompression"* |\n| **EMG** | *"**After 10 weeks have passed, the test is prognostic.** These action potentials can be found a week or two before the clinical evidence of recovery"* | **yes**, and the deck uses the word itself |\n\n**The two work at opposite ends of the illness, which is why the answer is both rather than either.** ENoG is an **early** predictor — it is read in the first days, while the nerve is still degenerating, and a figure above 90 % says recovery will be poor. EMG is a **late** one — after ten weeks it detects reinnervation potentials **one to two weeks before any visible movement returns**, so it predicts a recovery that has not yet happened. One forecasts failure early; the other confirms success early.\n\n**Why the singles fail:** choosing **B** alone would leave EMG, which the slide expressly calls prognostic, unchosen; choosing **C** alone would discard the 90 % rule that the deck uses to take a patient to theatre. **A** is excluded outright — the Hilger test is a bedside qualitative comparison and its window closes at 72 hours.\n\n**Bottom line: Hilger = is the nerve still conducting? ENoG = how much has degenerated, and how badly will it do? EMG = has reinnervation begun yet?** Compare `enthd-ear-123`, the same option list asked for the **quantitative** test, where ENoG stands alone. Cross-references: `entqb-ear5-137`, `entqb-ear5-138`, `entqb-ear5-142`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'ENoG is prognostic early (>90 % degeneration means poor recovery) and EMG is prognostic late (reinnervation potentials precede visible movement by 1–2 weeks); the Hilger test is neither.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.50 (Ear ch.6). *"Degeneration of more than 90% means recovery is poor"* and *"After 10 weeks have passed, the test is prognostic"* are transcribed verbatim from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`; the framing of the two as early-versus-late predictors is added and is not on the slide. **Paired with `enthd-ear-123` — identical options, one adjective changed, different key.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-125', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'A **lesion in the pons** will have all of the following findings **except**?',
  options:['Abducent palsy','LMNL facial palsy','Loss of lacrimation','Normal taste and salivation'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Loss of lacrimation — a nuclear lesion sits ABOVE the point at which the secretomotor fibres join the nerve, so lacrimation, salivation and taste are all preserved.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` states it in one sentence: *"i- **Nuclear lesions** are associated by **crossed hemiplegia, crossed hemianesthesia, other cranial nerves palsies specially VI. Lacrimation, salivation and taste are normal.**"*\n\n**The anatomy behind that sentence.** The deck lists **three separate nuclei** feeding the facial nerve: *"A- The **motor nucleus**: in the pons… B- The **superior salivatory nucleus**: parasympathetic secretory fibers to **lacrimal glands**, nasal glands, submandibular, sublingual and palatal salivary glands. C- The **nucleus of the tractus solitarius**: receives taste sensation."* A lesion confined to the **motor** nucleus destroys the motor supply only; the secretomotor and taste fibres, arising from their own nuclei and running as the **nervus intermedius**, are untouched. They will only be lost once they have joined the trunk — which happens further along, and is why **lacrimation is the marker of a lesion at or above the geniculate ganglion** (`enthd-ear-138`).\n\n| Option | Why it IS found in a pontine lesion |\n|---|---|\n| **Abducent palsy** | the deck names it specifically — *"other cranial nerves palsies **specially VI**"*. The VI nucleus lies in the pons and the facial motor fibres loop **around** it, so the two are struck together; a convergent squint beside a facial palsy is the classic pontine sign |\n| **LMNL facial palsy** | the lesion is at the **lower motor neurone cell body**, so the palsy is flaccid and takes the **whole** face — the deck’s LMNL column reads *"Flaccid / Whole face"*. A pontine lesion is anatomically central but clinically a lower motor neurone palsy |\n| **Loss of lacrimation** | **keyed as the exception** — the slide says lacrimation is normal |\n| **Normal taste and salivation** | again the slide’s own words, for the same reason: separate nuclei, fibres not yet joined |\n\n**⚠️ Note the shape of the option list.** Options C and D are the two halves of one sentence pulled apart and given opposite truth values; the item is testing whether the sentence is known, not whether it can be reasoned out. **Learn it as a block: nuclear = crossed hemiplegia + VI palsy + whole-face flaccid palsy, with lacrimation, salivation and taste ALL NORMAL.**\n\nCross-references: `entqb-ear5-128` (facial weakness with contralateral hemiplegia and squint — the same lesion asked as a localisation), `entqb-ear5-127`, `enthd-ear-138`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A pontine (nuclear) facial lesion gives a whole-face flaccid palsy with crossed hemiplegia and a VI nerve palsy, but lacrimation, salivation and taste are normal because those fibres arise from separate nuclei.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.50 (Ear ch.6). The nuclear-lesion sentence, the three nuclei and the UMNL/LMNL table are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`; the loop of the facial fibres around the abducent nucleus is added and is not on the slide. Cross-bank companion: `entqb-ear5-128`.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-ear-126', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'A **40-year-old patient with a normal tympanic membrane and with no history of trauma** complains of **inability to close the eye**. What’s the most likely diagnosis?',
  options:['Bell’s palsy','Ramsy hunt syndrome','LMNL Facial palsy','None of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Bell’s palsy — because the stem has already excluded, one clause at a time, everything that a facial palsy is normally excluded against.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` defines it as *"the **sudden occurrence of a flaccid facial paralysis (LMNL) in an apparently healthy individual**. It is the commonest cause of intratemporal causes of facial paralysis… **It is the most common form of FN paralysis**"*, incidence *"1/5000 per year"*, and — the line this question is built on — *"**Diagnosis: It’s a diagnosis of exclusion.**"*\n\n**Read the stem as an exclusion list against the deck’s own cause list:**\n\n| The deck’s infranuclear causes | Excluded by |\n|---|---|\n| *"Inflammatory: Malignant ext. otitis; **AOM, Ch.OM, cholesteatoma**"* | **normal tympanic membrane** — every one of these shows on otoscopy |\n| *"Traumatic: **Fracture base**, forceps delivery"* | **no history of trauma** |\n| *"Herpes zoster oticus (Ramasy Hunt syndrome)"* | no vesicles and no severe otalgia described |\n| *"Congenital: poor facial muscular development"* | age 40, sudden onset |\n| *"**Idiopathic: Bell’s palsy**"* | **nothing excludes it — so it is the answer** |\n\n| Option | Why it is not the key |\n|---|---|\n| **Bell’s palsy** | **keyed** — the residue after exclusion, and the commonest cause anyway |\n| **Ramsay Hunt syndrome** | needs the rash. The deck’s triad is *"Facial paralysis; **Auditory and vestibular dysfunction** associated with **Painful herpetic vesicles of the auricle and the EAC**"*. None of the three additional features is present, and the drum is normal |\n| **LMNL facial palsy** | **true but not a diagnosis.** It is the *category* Bell’s palsy belongs to — the deck’s own classification is supra-nuclear, nuclear and infra-nuclear (LMNL), and every cause in the table above is an LMNL. The stem asks *what is the diagnosis*, and naming the level of the lesion answers a different question |\n| **None of the above** | fails, since A is available |\n\n**⚠️ A defect in the printed stem, recorded and not disputed.** The book prints *"inability of **opening** eye"*. A facial palsy paralyses **orbicularis oculi** and therefore abolishes eye **closure**; inability to *open* the eye is **ptosis** — levator palpebrae superioris, a third-nerve or sympathetic sign — which does not belong to this picture at all. Read as written the stem describes a different disease; read as intended, *cannot close the eye*, it is the classic sign, and the key is unaffected. **The stem is corrected here and the printing is recorded.** The endpoint bank asks the fact the right way round in `entep-ear-186`, *"failure to close the eye voluntarily"*, keyed to LMNL.\n\n**Bottom line: a sudden unilateral LMN facial palsy with a normal ear, no rash and no trauma is Bell’s palsy — after you have looked at the drum, the parotid and the other cranial nerves and found nothing.** Cross-references: `entep-ear-229` (commonest cause of facial paralysis, keyed Bell’s), `entep-ear-251`, `entqb-ear5-136`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Bell’s palsy is a diagnosis of exclusion — a sudden LMN facial palsy in a healthy adult with a normal drum, no trauma and no vesicles.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.51 (Ear ch.6). **⚠️ Source defect recorded, key held: the printed stem reads "inability of opening eye", where a facial palsy causes failure to CLOSE the eye — corrected in the stem above.** The definition of Bell’s palsy, its incidence, "diagnosis of exclusion" and the infranuclear cause list are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`. Cross-bank agreement with `entep-ear-229`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-127', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'**Facial tics due to cross innervation** occur in which of the following cases?',
  options:['Neuropraxia','Axonotmesis','Neurotmesis','All of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Neurotmesis — cross innervation requires axons to regrow into the wrong tubes, and only the grade in which every sheath is torn allows that.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` files the symptom precisely: under *"B- Complications due to **abnormal regeneration**"* it lists *"1. **Facial tics and spasms: due to short-circuiting between nerve fibers**. 2. Abnormal mass movement of the face: **Synkinesis**. 3. **Crocodile tears**: due to misdirected salivation fibers to the lacrimal gland. 4. **Frey’s syndrome**: facial flushing and sweating over the parotid during mastication = misdirected salivary fibers to the sweat glands."* Every item on that list is a **misrouting** phenomenon.\n\n**So the question is really: which grade lets a regrowing axon end up in the wrong place?** The deck’s three grades answer it:\n\n| Grade | The deck’s own words | Can axons be misrouted? |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Neuropraxia** | *"**Physiologic block, No anatomical disruption**, temporary, lasts few days and full return of function is expected"* — the axon is intact and *"the nerve fiber distal to the site of injury retains normal electrical response"* | **No.** Nothing regrows because nothing was cut |\n| **Axonotmesis** | *"**Axon sheath is intact but the axon is divided**… Distal degeneration (Wallerian) occurs. **Epineurium is intact**"* | Regrowth happens, but the surviving sheath guides each axon back down **its own** tube — recovery is orderly |\n| **Neurotmesis** | *"**Epineurium enclosing all nerve fascicles is also torn**. Regeneration is the worst and **traumatic neuroma may develop**"* | **Yes — keyed.** With the scaffolding destroyed, sprouting axons enter whichever tube they meet: a motor axon meant for the orbicularis oris reaches the orbicularis oculi (synkinesis, tics), a salivary secretomotor fibre reaches the lacrimal gland (crocodile tears) or a sweat gland (Frey’s) |\n| **All of the above** | fails on neuropraxia alone, where no degeneration and therefore no regeneration occurs |\n\n**⚠️ One nuance the option list hides, recorded rather than used to dispute the key.** The deck’s own Seddon/Sunderland table puts **Sunderland III and IV under the heading "Axonotmesis"** — *"III Axons and **endoneurium** disrupted; IV Axons, endoneurium, & **perineurium** disrupted"*. Once the **endoneurial tube** is broken the guidance is lost, so aberrant regeneration is possible from grade III upward, not only in complete transection *(this refinement is not stated on the slide)*. Seddon’s three-word scheme, which the options use, cannot express that — and within the three words offered, **neurotmesis is the correct one.**\n\n**Bottom line: no cut, no misdirection. The complications of abnormal regeneration are the price of a nerve that had to find its own way back.** Compare `enthd-ear-128` and `enthd-ear-135`, which ask the same three grades by definition. Cross-references: `entqb-ear5-123`, `entqb-ear5-124`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Facial tics, synkinesis, crocodile tears and Frey’s syndrome are all aberrant regeneration — they need the nerve sheaths destroyed, i.e. neurotmesis; neuropraxia never regenerates at all.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.51 (Ear ch.6). The abnormal-regeneration complication list, the three Seddon grades and the Seddon/Sunderland table are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`; **the observation that Sunderland III–IV sit inside "axonotmesis" yet already lose endoneurial guidance is added and is not on the slide — recorded as a nuance, the key is not disputed.** Cross-reference: `entqb-ear5-123`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-128', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'Which of the following terms refers to **axon degeneration with loss of nerve continuity**?',
  options:['Neuropraxia','Axonotemesis','Neurontemesis','All of the above','None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Neurotmesis — the key word in the stem is NERVE continuity, not axon continuity, and only neurotmesis divides the nerve itself.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` gives the three grades in a single block, and the difference between the last two is exactly what this question tests:\n\n| Grade | Slide wording | What has lost continuity |\n|---|---|---|\n| **1. Neuropraxia** | *"**Physiologic block, No anatomical disruption**, temporary, lasts few days and full return of function is expected. The axoplasm can still be transported… and so the nerve fiber distal to the site of injury retains **normal electrical response**"* | **nothing** — there is no degeneration at all |\n| **2. Axonotmesis** | *"**Axon sheath is intact but the axon is divided**. i.e. more severe lesion. **Distal degeneration (Wallerian) occurs. Epineurium is intact**"* | the **axon** only. The nerve trunk is still one continuous structure |\n| **3. Neurotmesis** | *"**Epineurium enclosing all nerve fascicles is also torn.** Regeneration is the worst and **traumatic neuroma may develop**"*, and in the Sunderland column *"V **Complete transection**"* | the **whole nerve** — with Wallerian degeneration distally as well |\n\n**So the stem describes two things at once**: degeneration of the axon (which axonotmesis and neurotmesis share) **plus** loss of continuity of the nerve (which only neurotmesis has). Axonotmesis is the trap precisely because it satisfies the first half of the sentence and fails the second — the epineurium is expressly stated to be intact.\n\n**Why the remaining options fail:** **neuropraxia** has neither feature; it is a conduction block in an anatomically whole nerve, which is why the distal segment still responds to stimulation. **All of the above** would make neuropraxia a transection. **None of the above** is unavailable once option C fits the definition word for word.\n\n**⚠️ Five options are printed here, A–E**, and the book spells the terms *"Axonotemesis"* and *"Neurontemesis"*; the misspellings are the source’s and are preserved in the option list above.\n\n**Bottom line: -praxia = blocked, -tmesis = cut. Axonotmesis cuts the axon; neurotmesis cuts the nerve — and that is why one recovers well and the other grows a traumatic neuroma.** Compare `enthd-ear-135` (the same three grades keyed to axonotmesis by its definition) and `enthd-ear-136` (the Sunderland numbers). Cross-references: `entqb-ear5-123` (which injury has the worst prognosis), `entqb-ear5-124`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Neurotmesis is loss of continuity of the whole nerve — epineurium torn, complete transection, worst regeneration; axonotmesis divides only the axon and leaves the sheaths intact.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.51 (Ear ch.6). **⚠️ Five printed options, A–E; the spellings "Axonotemesis" and "Neurontemesis" are the source’s and are preserved.** The three Seddon definitions and the Sunderland table are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`. Sibling items in this batch: `enthd-ear-127`, `enthd-ear-135`, `enthd-ear-136`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-129', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'The clinical findings in **Ramsay-Hunt syndrome** include the following **except**?',
  options:['Lower motor neuron facial paralysis','Eruption of vesicles on the auricle','Tympanic membrane bullae','Vertigo'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Tympanic membrane bullae — the deck’s definition of the syndrome names the auricle and the external canal, not the drum, and "bullae on the drum" is the language of a different disease.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` prints the syndrome as a triad: *"**Ramsay Hunt Syndrome consists of: Facial paralysis; Auditory and vestibular dysfunction associated with Painful herpetic vesicles of the auricle and the EAC.**"* It adds that *"Vesicles may also affect **palate & anterior 2/3 of tongue**"* — the distribution of the **nervus intermedius** and the **chorda tympani**, i.e. the sensory and taste territory of the facial nerve — and lists it among the inflammatory causes of infranuclear palsy as *"**Herpes zoster oticus** (Ramasy Hunt syndrome)"*.\n\n| Option | In the syndrome? | Grounding |\n|---|---|---|\n| **LMN facial paralysis** | **yes** | the first element of the triad; the virus reactivates in the geniculate ganglion, which lies **on** the facial nerve, so the palsy takes the whole half of the face |\n| **Vesicles on the auricle** | **yes** | *"painful herpetic vesicles of the **auricle** and the EAC"* — verbatim |\n| **Tympanic membrane bullae** | **keyed as the exception** | the drum is not in the deck’s list. **Haemorrhagic bullae on the tympanic membrane are the sign of bullous myringitis**, an acute viral or mycoplasmal myringitis presenting with severe otalgia and blood-stained discharge — *no facial palsy, no vertigo* *(bullous myringitis is not named on any cached ENT slide — not taken from the course material)* |\n| **Vertigo** | **yes** | the triad’s *"auditory and **vestibular** dysfunction"*; the eighth nerve runs beside the seventh in the internal auditory canal, so SNHL and vertigo accompany the palsy |\n\n**⚠️ A caveat worth recording, which does not move the key.** In clinical practice zoster vesicles are described on the tympanic membrane in some cases, since the nervus intermedius supplies part of it — so the finding is not impossible in the disease *(not taken from the course material)*. The question is nevertheless answerable as printed, because it asks which finding does **not** belong to the syndrome as taught, and the deck’s own definition confines the rash to the **auricle, EAC, palate and anterior two-thirds of the tongue**. **The word "bullae" also points elsewhere: zoster gives grouped painful *vesicles*, myringitis gives *bullae*.**\n\n**Bottom line: Ramsay Hunt = palsy + painful auricular/canal vesicles + eighth-nerve symptoms. Blisters on the drum with a normal face is bullous myringitis.** Cross-references: `entep-ear-55` (the same "except" question, where **pharyngeal paralysis** is the exception), `entqb-ear5-135`, `entep-ear-207`, `entep-mfe5-30`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Ramsay Hunt syndrome is LMN facial palsy with painful vesicles of the auricle and external canal and eighth-nerve symptoms; bullae on the tympanic membrane belong to bullous myringitis.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.51 (Ear ch.6). The triad, the palate and tongue extension, and herpes zoster oticus among the inflammatory causes are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`. **Bullous myringitis appears on no cached ENT slide and is supplied from general knowledge, tagged in the explanation; the caveat that zoster vesicles can reach the drum is recorded there too — the key is held as printed.** Cross-bank sibling: `entep-ear-55`, the same stem with a different exception.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-ear-130', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'Which of the following **differentiates Ramsay Hunt syndrome from Bell’s palsy**?',
  options:['Severe burning sensation in the ears + recovery is not the rule in Ramsy Hunt syndrome','Severe itchy vesicles on the auricle in Ramsy Hunt syndrome','UMNL not LMNL in Ramsy Hunt syndrome','All of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Option A — and it is the second half of it, the prognosis, that actually does the work.**\n\n**The prognostic gap is the deck’s own, and it is wide.** `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` gives Bell’s palsy *"**71% complete recovery; 25% recovers with slight sequalae; and 4% with severe sequelae. Not a single patient who does not show some recovery.**"* Against that it gives herpes zoster oticus *"**30-50% show incomplete recovery**"*, *"**The FN appears to be more severely damaged in herpes zoster oticus than with idiopathic facial palsy. Only 30% of patients will recover normal function, and thus 70% experience some sequelae.**"* So in Bell’s palsy **recovery is the rule** — every patient shows some — while in Ramsay Hunt it is not.\n\n**The pain half is true but weaker.** The deck calls the Ramsay Hunt eruption *"**painful** herpetic vesicles of the auricle and the EAC"*, and the syndrome is preceded by severe otalgia. But Bell’s palsy is not painless: the same deck records *"**Pain in the ear is noted in over half of patients**"*. Pain therefore shifts the odds; it does not separate the two. **The word "burning" appears on no cached ENT slide** *(not taken from the course material — burning, lancinating pain is the usual description of zoster prodrome)*. Read the option as a package: it is keyed because its prognostic clause is unarguable.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **A — burning ear pain + recovery not the rule** | **keyed.** Prognosis is the hard discriminator: 100 % show some recovery in Bell’s, 70 % have sequelae in zoster |\n| **B — severe ITCHY vesicles** | the vesicles are the single most reliable clinical difference — Bell’s has none — but the deck twice calls them **painful**, not itchy. **The option is spoiled by one adjective**, which is exactly how this bank builds a near-miss |\n| **C — UMNL not LMNL** | **false.** Both are lower motor neurone palsies. The deck lists *"Herpes zoster oticus (Ramasy Hunt syndrome)"* under **infra-nuclear** inflammatory causes, and Bell’s under *"Idiopathic"* in the same infra-nuclear list. Neither spares the forehead |\n| **D — all of the above** | fails with B and C |\n\n**⚠️ Treatment differs too, and the difference tracks the prognosis** — Ramsay Hunt gets *"Acyclovir (Zovirax)… 800 mg 5 times daily for a week"* **plus** *"Oral steroids 1m/kg/day for **2 weeks**"* and *"Decompression if degeneration is more than 90% in **2 weeks**"*, against Bell’s palsy’s one week of aciclovir with cortisone and decompression at more than 90 % degeneration **within 6 days**.\n\n**Bottom line: vesicles make the diagnosis, the prognosis makes the difference.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear5-146` (Grade Gain asks this comparison directly), `entep-ear-55`, `entqb-ear5-148`, and `enthd-ear-129` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Bell’s palsy always shows some recovery (71 % complete); herpes zoster oticus leaves 70 % with sequelae — and both are lower motor neurone palsies, so the UMNL claim is simply false.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.51 (Ear ch.6). Both prognosis blocks, *"Pain in the ear is noted in over half of patients"*, the *"painful herpetic vesicles"* wording and both treatment regimens are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`. **The descriptor "burning" is on no cached slide and is tagged in the explanation; option B is recorded as spoiled by the single word "itchy" — the key is held.** Cross-bank companion: `entqb-ear5-146`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-131', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'**Deviation of the angle of the mouth, intact eye lid movement and normal emotional face movement** are suggestive of which of the following conditions?',
  options:['Upper motor neuron facial paralysis','Parkinsonism','Lower motor neuron facial paralysis','Paralysis of the mandibular branch of the facial nerve after parotid'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Upper motor neuron paralysis — the lower face is weak, the eye is spared, and the emotional smile still works. All three facts are supranuclear signatures.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` sets the first two out in its comparison table:\n\n| Difference | Supra-nuclear (UMNL) | Infra-nuclear (LMNL) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Type of paralysis | **Spastic** | Flaccid |\n| **Distribution** | **Lower face** | **Whole face** |\n| Other signs | **Crossed hemiplegia** | — |\n\nand the third is why the deck instructs the examiner to test both kinds of movement: *"Testing for **facial movement and emotional movement**. (Laughing; blinking, wrinkling, closing eyes, whistling, grinning and blowing of the cheeks)."*\n\n**Why the eye is spared** *(the bilateral cortical supply is stated on the slide only for the nucleus, and the clinical consequence is added here)*: the deck says the motor nucleus’s *"**Sup. Part: bilateral innervations from the motor cortex, inferior part: unilateral crossed cortical innervations**"*. The upper part of the nucleus drives the forehead and orbicularis oculi and receives fibres from **both** hemispheres, so one cortical lesion leaves it covered by the other side. The lower part has only crossed input, so it fails — and the mouth droops alone.\n\n**Why the emotional smile survives.** Volitional movement runs down the corticobulbar tract; **emotional movement is driven by extrapyramidal pathways that bypass the damaged corticobulbar fibres** *(not taken from the course material)*. A patient who cannot bare their teeth to command may smile symmetrically at a joke — the dissociation the deck is testing for when it asks for both.\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Parkinsonism** | the **mirror-image dissociation**: a mask-like, hypomimic face in which **spontaneous emotional expression is lost** while voluntary movement is preserved on command, and it is **bilateral and symmetrical** — no deviation of the mouth *(not taken from the course material; no cached ENT deck covers Parkinsonism)* |\n| **LMN facial paralysis** | takes the **whole** face by the deck’s own table, so the eye would not close and the emotional smile would be lost as well. Its cardinal sign is precisely the one this stem denies |\n| **Mandibular branch injury after parotid surgery** | **the strongest distractor, and it is excluded by one clause.** Cutting the marginal mandibular branch does deviate the angle of the mouth with a normally closing eye — but the branch is the **final common path** for that muscle, so the **emotional** smile would be lost on that side too. Only a supranuclear lesion spares emotional movement. `L15` lists *"Iatrogenic: mastoid, **parotid**, CPA surgery"* among the causes, so the mechanism is real; the sign pattern is wrong |\n\n**Bottom line: droop below, eye above, emotion intact = above the nucleus. Test the smile you did not ask for.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear5-126` (normal eye occlusion with the mouth turned — the same discrimination), `entqb-ear5-134`, `entqb-ear5-152`, `entep-ear-215`, and `enthd-ear-132` in this batch, which asks the same table the other way round.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'An upper motor neurone facial palsy weakens the lower face only, spares eye closure through the bilateral cortical supply of the upper nucleus, and spares emotional movement, which runs on extrapyramidal pathways.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.52 (Ear ch.6). The UMNL/LMNL table, the instruction to test facial **and** emotional movement, the bilateral innervation of the superior part of the motor nucleus and *"Iatrogenic: mastoid, parotid, CPA surgery"* are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`. **The extrapyramidal route of emotional movement and the hypomimia of Parkinsonism are supplied and tagged — no cached ENT deck covers either.** Paired with `enthd-ear-132`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-132', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'**Upper motor neuron facial nerve paralysis** is characterized by the following **except**…?',
  options:['Intact eye closure','Intact emotional face movement','Intact voluntary face movement','Intact gustatory sensation'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Intact voluntary face movement — that is the one thing a supranuclear lesion destroys.** Everything else in the list survives it.\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` gives the distribution in one word: the supra-nuclear column reads *"Distribution — **Lower face**"*, against *"Whole face"* for the infra-nuclear lesion. What is lost in the lower face is **voluntary** movement to command: grinning, whistling, blowing out the cheeks, baring the teeth. **The corticobulbar tract is the voluntary pathway, and it is the pathway that has been cut.**\n\n| Option | Intact in UMNL? | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Eye closure** | **yes** | *"Sup. Part: **bilateral innervations from the motor cortex**, inferior part: unilateral crossed cortical innervations"* — the upper half of the nucleus is covered by the other hemisphere, so the forehead and orbicularis oculi work |\n| **Emotional face movement** | **yes** | the deck asks the examiner to test *"facial movement **and emotional movement**"* precisely because the two can dissociate; emotional expression travels extrapyramidal routes that bypass the corticobulbar lesion *(not taken from the course material)* |\n| **Voluntary face movement** | **NO — keyed** | the corticobulbar supply to the lower face is unilateral and crossed, so it fails outright |\n| **Gustatory sensation** | **yes** | taste is carried by the **chorda tympani** to *"the nucleus of the tractus solitarius"*, which is a **brainstem** nucleus. A lesion above the nucleus cannot touch it — the deck makes the same point about nuclear lesions, where *"lacrimation, salivation and taste are normal"* |\n\n**⚠️ The stem needs reading twice.** Three of the four options begin *intact*, and the answer is the one that is **not** intact — an "except" question layered on top of a list of preserved functions. **Translate it first: which function is LOST in an upper motor neuron facial palsy?**\n\n**Bottom line: UMNL loses voluntary lower-face movement and nothing else — not the eye, not the smile, not taste.** That is why the eye is the bedside test: an eye that will not close is a **lower** motor neurone lesion (`entep-ear-186`). Cross-references: `entep-ear-215`, `entqb-ear5-134`, `entqb-ear5-152`, and `enthd-ear-131` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'An upper motor neurone facial palsy abolishes voluntary movement of the contralateral lower face only — eye closure, emotional movement and taste are all preserved.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.52 (Ear ch.6). The UMNL/LMNL distribution table, the bilateral innervation of the superior part of the motor nucleus, the nucleus of the tractus solitarius receiving taste and *"lacrimation, salivation and taste are normal"* in nuclear lesions are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`; the extrapyramidal route of emotional movement is tagged as outside the course material. Paired with `enthd-ear-131`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-135', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'**Interruption of axon and endoneurium** refers to which of the following?',
  options:['Neuropraxia','Axonotmesis','Neurotmesis','None of the above'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Axonotmesis — because the deck’s own Seddon/Sunderland table puts "axons and endoneurium disrupted" inside the axonotmesis band.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` prints the table that decides this question:\n\n| Seddon | Sunderland | Pathophysiologic process |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Neuropraxia** | **I** | Physiologic block |\n| **Axonotmesis** | **II** | Axons disrupted |\n| | **III** | **Axons and endoneurium disrupted** |\n| | **IV** | Axons, endoneurium, & perineurium disrupted |\n| **Neurotmesis** | **V** | Complete transection |\n\nThe stem’s wording — *interruption of axon and endoneurium* — is **Sunderland III word for word**, and Sunderland III, IV and II all sit under the single Seddon heading **axonotmesis**. Since the options are Seddon’s three terms, the answer is axonotmesis.\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Neuropraxia** | *"Physiologic block, **No anatomical disruption**"* — Sunderland I. Nothing is interrupted at all; the distal fibre *"retains normal electrical response"* |\n| **Axonotmesis** | **keyed** — Sunderland II–IV, of which III is the stem |\n| **Neurotmesis** | Sunderland V, *"complete transection"*, in which *"the **epineurium** enclosing all nerve fascicles is also torn"*. The stem stops at the endoneurium and says nothing about perineurium or epineurium |\n| **None of the above** | unavailable once the table names the grade |\n\n**⚠️ A genuine tension inside the source, recorded and not disputed.** The deck’s **prose** definition of axonotmesis reads *"**Axon sheath is intact** but the axon is divided… Epineurium is intact"*, which describes Sunderland **II** and would exclude an interrupted endoneurium. Its **table**, on the same deck, expressly includes *"axons and endoneurium disrupted"* under axonotmesis. **The prose is Seddon’s original narrow definition; the table is the Sunderland refinement grafted onto it, and the bank keys from the table.** Both are the source’s and both are recorded here; the key stands.\n\n**Why the distinction is not academic.** As soon as the **endoneurial tube** is breached the regrowing axon loses the sleeve that would return it to its own muscle — which is the mechanism behind synkinesis, facial tics and crocodile tears (`enthd-ear-127`). **Sunderland III is where recovery stops being reliably complete.**\n\nCompare `enthd-ear-136`, which asks the same row by its Sunderland number, and `enthd-ear-128`, which asks for loss of continuity of the whole nerve. Cross-references: `entqb-ear5-123`, `entqb-ear5-124`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Axon plus endoneurium disrupted is Sunderland III, which the deck files under the Seddon heading axonotmesis; neurotmesis requires the epineurium to be torn.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.52 (Ear ch.6). The Seddon/Sunderland table and the three prose definitions are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`. **⚠️ Source tension recorded, key held: the deck’s prose says axonotmesis leaves the axon sheath intact, while its table places "axons and endoneurium disrupted" under axonotmesis — the bank keys from the table.** The consequence for aberrant regeneration is added and is not on the slide. Siblings in this batch: `enthd-ear-127`, `enthd-ear-128`, `enthd-ear-136`. **Q133 and Q134 are not in this batch.**' }
,

{ id:'enthd-ear-136', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'**Sunderland stage III** of nerve injury corresponds to which of the following?',
  options:['Disruption of axons and epineurium','Disruption of axons and endoneurium','Disruption of axons and perineurium','Complete transection of nerve fiber'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Sunderland III is axons plus endoneurium — the deck prints the row verbatim.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` carries the five-stage table beside its Seddon equivalents:\n\n| Seddon | Sunderland | Pathophysiologic process |\n|---|---|---|\n| Neuropraxia | **I** | Physiologic block |\n| Axonotmesis | **II** | Axons disrupted |\n| | **III** | **Axons and endoneurium disrupted** |\n| | **IV** | Axons, endoneurium, & perineurium disrupted |\n| Neurotmesis | **V** | Complete transection |\n\nand restates the same sequence in prose: *"2. Second degree/ Axonotmesis. -The axons continuity are disrupted. 3. **Third degree. -The endoneurium are disrupted.** 4. Forth degree. -The **perineum** surrounding the nerve fascicles are disrupted."*\n\n**The three sheaths go from inside out, and the numbers count outward.** Endoneurium wraps the **individual axon**; perineurium wraps a **fascicle**; epineurium wraps the **whole nerve**. So stage II reaches the axon, III the endoneurium, IV the perineurium, and V takes everything, epineurium included.\n\n| Option | Which stage it actually is |\n|---|---|\n| **Axons and epineurium** | not a Sunderland stage at all — the epineurium is the **outermost** sheath and cannot be torn while perineurium and endoneurium survive. A distractor built from the right words in an impossible order |\n| **Axons and endoneurium** | **keyed — stage III** |\n| **Axons and perineurium** | the beginning of **stage IV**, which the table writes as *axons, endoneurium **and** perineurium* — the perineurium cannot go without the endoneurium going first |\n| **Complete transection** | **stage V**, Seddon’s neurotmesis |\n\n**Why the number matters clinically:** **stage II recovers well**, because each axon is still inside its own endoneurial tube and grows back to its own muscle. From **stage III** the tube is gone, so axons regrow into whatever tube they meet — the origin of synkinesis, facial tics and crocodile tears (`enthd-ear-127`).\n\nCompare `enthd-ear-135`, which asks the same row by its description, and `enthd-ear-128`. Cross-references: `entqb-ear5-124` (Grade Gain asks what a *grade 4* injury damages), `entqb-ear5-123`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Sunderland stages count outward through the sheaths: II axon, III axon + endoneurium, IV + perineurium, V complete transection — and recovery stops being reliable at III.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.53 (Ear ch.6). The Seddon/Sunderland table and the second-to-fourth-degree prose (which prints "perineum" for perineurium) are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`; the inside-out description of the three sheaths and the recovery consequence are added and are not on the slide. Siblings in this batch: `enthd-ear-127`, `enthd-ear-128`, `enthd-ear-135`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-137', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'Which of the following is the **most important part in the treatment of Bell’s palsy**?',
  options:['Acyclovir','CST','Eye lubricants','Antibiotics'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Eye lubricants — because the nerve recovers on its own and the cornea does not.**\n\n**The prognosis is what makes this the answer.** `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` gives Bell’s palsy *"**71% complete recovery; 25% recovers with slight sequalae; and 4% with severe sequelae. Not a single patient who does not show some recovery.**"*, notes that *"**Recovery starts even without treatment in most cases** within few days up to 3 weeks"*, and rates conservative management as *"**helpful in 96% of cases**"*. **A condition that recovers in every patient does not need to be rescued; the structure that will be permanently damaged does.**\n\n**And that structure is the eye.** With orbicularis oculi paralysed the lid will not close and the blink is lost, so the cornea dries and is unprotected. The deck lists the consequences under *"A- Complications due to FN paralysis"*: *"2- **Eye complication: exposure keratitis; corneal ulcer; panophthalmitis.**"* — a scarred cornea, or a lost eye, from a disease of the face. The treatment slide names the measures: *"**Dark eye glasses and artificial tears. Protection of the eye.**"*, and where the palsy is prolonged the complication slide adds *"**Gold weight** for protection of the cornea"*.\n\n| Option | Its real place in management |\n|---|---|\n| **Acyclovir** | genuinely indicated — the deck attributes Bell’s palsy to *"VIRAL causes (Activation of **Herpes Simplex**)"* and prescribes *"Zovirax (**800 mg 5 x daily for ONE week**)"*. But it is given **with** steroids as part of a package that only improves an already good outcome |\n| **CST — corticosteroid therapy** | the **mainstay drug**, and rational on the deck’s own pathogenesis: *"**Segmental demyelination followed by 2ry edema and compression**"* — reduce oedema inside a rigid bony canal and you relieve the compression. It is the strongest distractor and it is beaten only on the argument above |\n| **Eye lubricants** | **keyed** — the only measure that prevents an irreversible loss |\n| **Antibiotics** | **have no place at all.** Bell’s palsy is idiopathic and virally attributed; there is no bacterial infection to treat. Antibiotics belong to the *inflammatory* causes on the deck’s list — *"Malignant ext. otitis; AOM, Ch.OM"* — which are different diseases with a visibly abnormal ear |\n\n**⚠️ Read the question’s adjective, because the same topic has two different right answers.** Asked *what is the treatment*, the answer is aciclovir plus cortisone plus physiotherapy plus eye care — the endpoint bank asks it that way. Asked *what is the **most important** step, the answer changes to eye protection. **Grade Gain prints this exact question and keys it the same way**: `entqb-ear5-144`, options *"Administer antiviral medication / **Protect the eye** / Perform facial exercises / Start corticosteroids"*. **Two banks, two option lists, one key — the cross-bank agreement is what settles any doubt about this key.**\n\n**Bottom line: in a self-limiting nerve palsy, treat what will not recover. Ask what is permanently damaged if nothing is done — and it is the cornea.** ⚠️ The safety net is unchanged: complete paralysis with *"more than 90% degeneration by ENOG within 6 days"* goes for **decompression**, not for reassurance.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Bell’s palsy recovers in every patient and 96 % do well on conservative treatment — so the priority is not the nerve but the cornea: artificial tears, glasses, taping, and a gold weight if prolonged.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.53 (Ear ch.6). The prognosis figures, *"conservative treatment is helpful in 96% of cases"*, the aciclovir dose, the segmental-demyelination pathogenesis, the eye complications, *"dark eye glasses and artificial tears"*, the gold weight and the ENoG surgical threshold are all transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`. **⚠️ Key confirmed across banks: `entqb-ear5-144` prints the same question with "Protect the eye" against "Start corticosteroids" and keys the eye.** "CST" is the book’s abbreviation for corticosteroid therapy and is preserved in the option list.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-138', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'LMNL facial palsy **localized topographically to the geniculate ganglion** is characterized by which of the following?',
  options:['LMNL facial palsy, SNHL, vertigo & lost lacrimation','LMNL facial palsy, spared 8th CN & lost lacrimation','LMNL facial palsy, spared 8th CN & normal lacrimation','LMNL facial palsy, SNHL, vertigo & normal lacrimation'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Lacrimation lost, eighth nerve spared — two findings, and each fixes one end of the lesion.**\n\n**Lacrimation lost puts the lesion at or above the geniculate ganglion.** `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` lists the intratemporal branches in order — *"1- **Greater superficial petrosal (parasympathetic secretory)**, 2- Nerve to stapedius, 3- Chorda tympani (Taste)"* — and traces the segments: *"3- **Labyrinthine segment which ends by the geniculate ganglion**, 4- Tympanic (horizontal) segment, 5- Mastoid (vertical) segment which ends by the stylomastoid foramen"*. The greater superficial petrosal is the **first** branch and it leaves **at the ganglion**, carrying the secretomotor fibres that the deck traces to the *"**lacrimal glands**"* from the superior salivatory nucleus. A lesion below the ganglion therefore spares tearing; this one does not, so it is at the ganglion or higher. **Schirmer’s tear test is the first of the deck’s four topodiagnostic tests** for exactly this reason.\n\n**The eighth nerve spared stops the lesion going higher.** The seventh and eighth nerves travel together only through the **cerebellopontine angle and the internal auditory canal** — *"It arises from the inferior border of the pons (pontomedullary sulcus) **crosses the CPA to enter the IAC**"*. A lesion there would add sensorineural hearing loss and vertigo. Their absence drops it back to the ganglion.\n\n| Option | Where it would put the lesion |\n|---|---|\n| **SNHL, vertigo & lost lacrimation** | **IAC / CPA** — both nerves involved. That is the acoustic neuroma picture, not this one |\n| **Spared 8th CN & lost lacrimation** | **keyed — the geniculate ganglion**: below the canal, at or above the GSPN |\n| **Spared 8th CN & normal lacrimation** | **below** the ganglion — tympanic or mastoid segment, or the stylomastoid foramen if taste and the stapedial reflex are normal too |\n| **SNHL, vertigo & normal lacrimation** | **anatomically impossible.** The eighth nerve can only be caught proximal to the labyrinthine segment, which is proximal to the ganglion — so lacrimation could not be spared |\n\n**The topodiagnostic ladder, which answers every question of this family in one line:** *tears lost → at or above the geniculate; tears spared, stapedial reflex lost → mastoid segment; taste and salivation lost → chorda tympani; motor only → stylomastoid foramen; add an eighth nerve palsy → the canal.* The deck’s four tests map onto it exactly: *"1. **Schirmer’s tear test** 2. **Stapedius reflex** 3. **Evaluation of Taste** 4. **Submandibular salivary flow**"*.\n\n**⚠️ Clinically, the ganglion is where two named diseases sit** — **Ramsay Hunt syndrome**, in which the virus reactivates in the ganglion itself, and **transverse petrous fracture**, of which the deck says *"the site of the lesion is commonly at the **geniculate ganglion**"* and which accounts for facial palsy in *"40% of cases of transverse fractures"* against *"10% of cases of longitudinal fractures"*.\n\n**Cross-bank agreement:** Grade Gain asks the identical localisation and keys the geniculate ganglion (`entqb-ear5-130`), and the endpoint bank asks the higher-lesion half (`entep-ear-74`, lost lacrimation → internal auditory canal). Cross-references: `entqb-ear5-129`, `entqb-ear5-131`, `entqb-ear5-132`, `entep-ear-140`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The greater superficial petrosal leaves at the geniculate ganglion, so lost lacrimation with an intact eighth nerve localises a facial palsy exactly there.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.53 (Ear ch.6). The branch order, the five anatomical segments, the superior salivatory nucleus supplying the lacrimal gland, the four topodiagnostic tests, and the petrous-fracture figures (10 % longitudinal, 40 % transverse, lesion commonly at the geniculate ganglion) are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`. **Cross-bank agreement recorded with `entqb-ear5-130` and `entep-ear-74`.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-139', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'A patient with **painful vesicles in the concha, facial paralysis and deafness with vertigo**. What’s your diagnosis?',
  options:['Ramsay hunt','Otitis externa','Otitis interna','Facial paralysis'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Ramsay Hunt syndrome — the stem is the deck’s definition with the three elements in the order it prints them.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`: *"**Ramsay Hunt Syndrome consists of: Facial paralysis; Auditory and vestibular dysfunction associated with Painful herpetic vesicles of the auricle and the EAC.**"* Map the stem onto it: *painful vesicles in the concha* = the auricular rash (the concha is the deep bowl of the auricle, continuous with the canal — precisely the territory the deck names); *facial paralysis* = element one; *deafness with vertigo* = *"auditory and vestibular dysfunction"*.\n\n**The disease is herpes zoster oticus** — the deck lists it among the inflammatory infranuclear causes as *"**Herpes zoster oticus (Ramasy Hunt syndrome)**"*. The virus reactivates in the geniculate ganglion, the rash appears in the sensory territory the nervus intermedius supplies, and the eighth nerve is involved because it lies alongside the seventh.\n\n| Option | Why it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Otitis externa** | painful, yes — but a **diffuse inflammation of the canal skin** with no vesicles, **no facial palsy and no vertigo**. Note the deck’s careful wording elsewhere: only **malignant** external otitis causes facial paralysis, and that is a skull-base osteomyelitis in a diabetic, not this picture (`enthd-ear-140`) |\n| **Otitis interna** | i.e. labyrinthitis — this would explain the **deafness and vertigo** and nothing else. **No rash, no facial palsy.** It accounts for two findings out of four |\n| **Facial paralysis** | **the sign, offered as if it were the diagnosis** — the same trap as option C in `enthd-ear-126`. It restates one element of the stem and explains none of the others |\n\n**⚠️ The reason this diagnosis is worth making at the bedside is the prognosis.** The deck: *"The FN appears to be **more severely damaged in herpes zoster oticus** than with idiopathic facial palsy. **Only 30% of patients will recover normal function**, and thus **70% experience some sequelae**"* — against Bell’s palsy, where *"not a single patient… does not show some recovery"*. Treatment is correspondingly harder: *"Acyclovir (Zovirax)… 800 mg 5 times daily for a week"* **plus** *"Oral steroids 1m/kg/day for 2 weeks"*, and *"Decompression if degeneration is more than 90% in 2 weeks"*.\n\n**Bottom line: rash + palsy + eighth-nerve symptoms is one disease with one name. Look in the concha of every facial palsy.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear5-135`, `entep-ear-207`, `entep-mfe5-30`, `entep-enr-19`, `entep-ear-55`, and `enthd-ear-129`/`enthd-ear-130` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Painful auricular or canal vesicles with a lower motor neurone facial palsy and eighth-nerve symptoms is Ramsay Hunt syndrome — herpes zoster of the geniculate ganglion, with a far worse prognosis than Bell’s palsy.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.53 (Ear ch.6). The triad, herpes zoster oticus among the inflammatory infranuclear causes, the recovery figures and the treatment regimen are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`. The printed stem runs its clauses together without punctuation; commas are supplied above and the wording is otherwise unchanged. Cross-bank siblings: `entqb-ear5-135`, `entep-ear-207`, `entep-mfe5-30`, `entep-enr-19`.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-ear-140', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'The following are **causes of facial nerve paralysis except**?',
  options:['Acoustic neuroma','Harps zoster oticus','Facial nerve neuroma','Diffuse external otitis'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Diffuse external otitis — and the exception turns on one adjective, because the deck names MALIGNANT external otitis as a cause and not the ordinary diffuse kind.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` prints the infranuclear cause list, and three of the four options are on it word for word:\n\n> *"2. Inflammatory: **Malignant ext. otitis**; AOM, Ch.OM, cholesteatoma, **Herpes zoster oticus (Ramasy Hunt syndrome)**, spirochetes (Lyme disease)… 6. Neoplastic: 1ry tumor: **FN neuroma**; 2ry tumors of middle ear and parotid and **CPA**. 7. Iatrogenic: mastoid, parotid, **CPA surgery**."*\n\n| Option | On the list? | How it paralyses the nerve |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Acoustic neuroma** | **yes** | it is a **CPA tumour**, and the deck traces the nerve *"crosses the CPA to enter the IAC"*, listing *"Tumors of the CPA"* among causes. The seventh runs beside the eighth, so a schwannoma large enough to fill the angle compresses it |\n| **Herpes zoster oticus** | **yes** | named outright, with Ramsay Hunt syndrome beside it — the virus reactivates in the geniculate ganglion, on the nerve itself (`enthd-ear-139`) |\n| **Facial nerve neuroma** | **yes** | *"1ry tumor: FN neuroma"* — a schwannoma **of** the facial nerve. A slowly progressive palsy, which is the feature that separates it from Bell’s palsy |\n| **Diffuse external otitis** | **keyed as the exception** | a **cellulitis of the canal skin**. It is painful and it swells the canal, but it stays in the soft tissue and never reaches the nerve inside its bony canal |\n\n**⚠️ The trap, and it is worth spelling out.** *"Malignant" external otitis is not a cancer* — it is **skull-base osteomyelitis**, usually *Pseudomonas*, characteristically in an **elderly diabetic or immunocompromised** patient, spreading from the canal through the fissures of Santorini into the temporal bone. It reaches the **stylomastoid foramen** and the lower cranial nerves, which is why the deck lists it and why **a facial palsy in a diabetic with a painful ear is that disease until proved otherwise** *(the pathogen, the diabetic association and the route of spread are not on this deck and are not taken from the course material; `L17,18) Otorrhea and otalgia.pdf` sets an ILO to "relate the ear pain to the patient’s general condition to diagnose malignant otitis externa cases" but the cached text carries no detail)*. **A facial palsy converts an external otitis from a nuisance into an emergency.**\n\n**Bottom line: read the adjective. Diffuse external otitis is a skin infection; malignant external otitis is a bone infection, and only the second reaches the facial nerve.** Cross-references: `entep-ear-78` and `entep-ear-161` (the same "causes except" question in the endpoint bank), `entqb-ear5-154`, `entqb-ear5-143`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Malignant external otitis — skull-base osteomyelitis — causes facial palsy; ordinary diffuse external otitis, a cellulitis of the canal skin, does not.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.53 (Ear ch.6). **The book prints "Harps zoster oticus"; the misprint is preserved in the option list.** The infranuclear cause list, *"Malignant ext. otitis"*, *"1ry tumor: FN neuroma"*, CPA tumours and the nerve crossing the CPA are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`; the ILO on malignant otitis externa is from `L17,18) Otorrhea and otalgia.pdf`. **The pathology of malignant external otitis is supplied from general knowledge and tagged in the explanation — the cached decks name it without describing it.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-141', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'All of the following statements about **Bell’s palsy** are true **except**?',
  options:['Some patients don’t show any improvement despite adequate treatment','It is a form of lower motor neuron facial palsy','It is treated by Acyclovir','None of the above'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Option A is false, and it is false because of one sentence the deck prints in absolute terms.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`, on prognosis: *"**71% complete recovery; 25% recovers with slight sequalae; and 4% with severe sequelae. Not a single patient who does not show some recovery.**"*\n\nRead the three figures: 71 + 25 + 4 = **100 %**. There is no fourth category. The deck then says the same thing in words — **no patient fails to show some recovery** — and adds that *"**recovery starts even without treatment** in most cases within few days up to 3 weeks"*. So a Bell’s palsy that shows **no improvement whatever** is, by this deck’s teaching, not Bell’s palsy; it is a diagnosis to re-open.\n\n| Option | True? | Grounding |\n|---|---|---|\n| **No improvement despite adequate treatment** | **FALSE — keyed** | contradicted directly: *"not a single patient who does not show some recovery"* |\n| **A form of lower motor neuron facial palsy** | **true** | the definition itself: *"the sudden occurrence of a **flaccid facial paralysis (LMNL)** in an apparently healthy individual"*, and it is filed under **infra-nuclear** causes as *"8. Idiopathic: Bell’s palsy"* |\n| **Treated by acyclovir** | **true** | *"Zovirax (**800 mg 5 x daily for ONE week**) + Cortisone therapy"*, on the deck’s attribution of the disease to *"VIRAL causes (**Activation of Herpes Simplex**)"* |\n| **None of the above** | fails, since A is false | — |\n\n**⚠️ Note the distinction the option is trading on, and record it.** *Complete* recovery is **not** universal — a quarter are left with slight sequelae and 4 % with severe ones. The false claim is the stronger one: that **some show no improvement at all**. **The bank is testing whether the sentence was read, not whether the outcome is understood as perfect.**\n\n**And the practical corollary, which is the reason the sentence exists.** A facial palsy that does not begin to recover within about three weeks is being managed as something else: the deck’s own escalation is *"Surgical treatment: For complete paralysis with **more than 90% degeneration by ENOG within 6 days**: Decompression of the FN… through Middle Cranial Fossa"*, and its diagnostic caution is that Bell’s palsy *"is a **diagnosis of exclusion**"*. **No recovery means re-examine the ear, the parotid and the other cranial nerves, and image.**\n\nCross-references: `entqb-ear5-143` (what is NOT a cause of Bell’s palsy), `entqb-ear5-146` (its prognosis against herpes zoster oticus), `entep-ear-229`, and `enthd-ear-130`/`enthd-ear-137` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Every patient with Bell’s palsy shows some recovery — 71 % complete, 25 % slight sequelae, 4 % severe — so a palsy with no improvement at all is a diagnosis to reconsider, not a Bell’s palsy that failed.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.54 (Ear ch.6). The prognosis sentence, the LMNL definition, the herpes simplex attribution, the aciclovir dose, *"diagnosis of exclusion"* and the decompression criterion are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`. **The book prints "ture" for "true" in the stem; corrected above.** Cross-references verified: `entqb-ear5-143`, `entqb-ear5-146`, `entep-ear-229`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-142', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-facial',
  stem:'All of the following are among the **intratemporal branches of the facial nerve except**?',
  options:['Chorda tympani','Greater superficial petrosal nerve','Post auricular nerve','Nerve to Stapedius'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**The posterior auricular nerve — it is the FIRST branch given off after the nerve has left the temporal bone, so it is extratemporal by definition.**\n\n`L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf` divides the branches into two lists and the question is answered by reading them:\n\n| Group | The deck’s own list |\n|---|---|\n| **A- Intra-temporal branches** | *"1- **Greater superficial petrosal** (parasympathetic secretory); 2- **Nerve to stapedius muscle**; 3- **Chorda tympani** (Taste)"* |\n| **B- Extra-temporal branches** | *"1- **Post auricular nerve**; 2- Nerve to stylohyoid & posterior belly of digastric; 3- Cranio-temporal division; 4- Cranio-cervical division"* |\n\n**The dividing line is the stylomastoid foramen**, where the deck says the *"Mastoid (vertical) segment"* ends. Everything given off inside the bone is intratemporal; everything after the nerve emerges is extratemporal, and the posterior auricular nerve is the first of those.\n\n**Learn the three intratemporal branches in order, because they are also the topodiagnostic ladder:**\n\n| Branch | Leaves at | Function | Test |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Greater superficial petrosal** | the **geniculate ganglion** | lacrimation (and nasal/palatal glands) | **Schirmer’s tear test** |\n| **Nerve to stapedius** | tympanic/mastoid segment | damps the ossicular chain — its loss gives **hyperacusis** | **stapedius reflex** |\n| **Chorda tympani** | lower mastoid segment | **taste**, anterior two-thirds of tongue, and submandibular/sublingual salivation | **taste testing** and **submandibular salivary flow** |\n\nThose are exactly the deck’s four topodiagnostic tests — *"1. Schirmer’s tear test 2. Stapedius reflex 3. Evaluation of Taste 4. Submandibular salivary flow"* — which is why the branch list is worth knowing as anatomy **and** as localisation (`enthd-ear-138`).\n\n**Why each distractor is intratemporal:** **chorda tympani** literally crosses the middle ear, running between the incus and the malleus handle, and is seen at tympanoplasty; **greater superficial petrosal** leaves at the geniculate ganglion, deep inside the petrous bone; **nerve to stapedius** supplies a muscle that lives in the pyramidal eminence of the middle ear. **All three are inside the temporal bone by their very targets.**\n\nCross-references: `entqb-ear5-122` (the same "all are intratemporal except" question in Grade Gain), `entep-ear-174`, `entqb-ear5-145` (hyperacusis from the nerve to stapedius), `entqb-ear5-141` (testing the greater superficial petrosal).\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The intratemporal branches are the greater superficial petrosal, the nerve to stapedius and the chorda tympani; the posterior auricular nerve is the first extratemporal branch, given off beyond the stylomastoid foramen.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.54 (Ear ch.6). Both branch lists, the five anatomical segments and the four topodiagnostic tests are transcribed from `L15) Facial nerve disorders.pdf`; the course of the chorda tympani across the middle ear and the location of the stapedius in the pyramidal eminence are added and are not on the slide. **Held as a VARIANT of `entqb-ear5-122` and `entep-ear-174`**, which ask the same exclusion over different option sets.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-143', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'The following **PTA** demonstrates which **degree of hearing loss**? *(the book prints a pure-tone audiogram — threshold in dB against frequency in Hz)*',
  options:['Mild','Moderate','Severe','Profound'],
  answer:1,
  explanation:'**Moderate — which on this course means an average threshold between 40 and 60 dB.**\n\n`L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` prints the severity scale as a five-line list, and it is the whole of what these two questions test:\n\n| Degree | Threshold |\n|---|---|\n| **Normal** | **< 20 dB** |\n| **Mild** | **20–40 dB** |\n| **Moderate** | **40–60 dB** |\n| **Severe** | **60–90 dB** |\n| **Profound** | **90–110 dB** |\n| **Total** | **> 110 dB** |\n\nThe same deck defines what is being measured: pure tone audiometry is *"a **qualitative and quantitative** method"* which delivers *"sounds of variable frequencies and intensities to the ear via an earphones and a bone vibrator"* and records the **threshold of hearing** — *"the minimum intensity of sound necessary to be audible in at least 50% of presentations"*. The severity is read off that threshold; the **type** of loss is read off the relationship between the air and bone curves, which is a separate question (`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`: conductive = *"AC Abnormal, BC Normal"*, sensorineural = *"Abnormal **equal** AC and BC Thresholds"*, mixed = *"Abnormal **Unequal**"*).\n\n**⚠️ This item is decided entirely by the printed audiogram, and the figure cannot be re-read here.** The transcription recorded the axes, not the plotted thresholds; **no crop has been cut, so no `image` field is set.** The key is held as printed and the reasoning below is the method, not a reading of the graph.\n\n**How to answer it at the desk in three steps** *(the averaging convention is not stated on the cached slides and is not taken from the course material — the decks give the bands but not the arithmetic)*: take the **air conduction** thresholds of the worse ear, average the speech frequencies (500, 1000 and 2000 Hz), and drop the average into the table above. A curve sitting around the **50 dB** line is moderate; around **70–80 dB**, severe.\n\n| Option | Band | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Mild** | 20–40 dB | difficulty only with soft speech and in noise |\n| **Moderate** | **40–60 dB** | **keyed** — conversational speech is missed; the usual threshold for offering a hearing aid |\n| **Severe** | 60–90 dB | the key of `enthd-ear-144`, the identical question printed immediately below with a different audiogram |\n| **Profound** | 90–110 dB | the cochlear implant territory; the deck reserves *"deafness"* for *"profound or total hearing loss"* |\n\n**Bottom line: learn the four boundaries — 20, 40, 60, 90 — and every question of this family answers itself.** Cross-references: `enthd-ear-144` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'On this course a moderate hearing loss is a threshold of 40–60 dB; the boundaries to memorise are 20, 40, 60 and 90 dB.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.54 (Ear ch.6, but tested as audiology — filed to `ent-audio`). **The stem depends on a printed pure-tone audiogram; no crop exists yet, so no `image` field is set and the transcription recorded the axes, not the plotted thresholds. The key is held exactly as printed.** The severity bands and the definition of threshold are transcribed from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`; the AC/BC patterns from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`. **The three-frequency averaging convention is supplied and tagged — no cached deck states it.** Deliberately paired with `enthd-ear-144`, the same stem with a different figure and the key one band worse (severe).' }
,

{ id:'enthd-ear-144', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'The following **PTA** demonstrates which **degree of hearing loss**? *(the book prints a second pure-tone audiogram — threshold in dB against frequency in Hz)*',
  options:['Mild','Moderate','Severe','Profound'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Severe — 60 to 90 dB on this course’s scale.**\n\nThe severity table and the method for reading it are set out in full in `enthd-ear-143`, the twin item printed immediately above with the identical four options and a different audiogram; they are not repeated here. The one line that decides this question is `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`: *"**3- Severe: 60-90 dB**"*, sitting between *"2- Moderate: 40-60 dB"* and *"4- Profound: 90-110 dB"*.\n\n**⚠️ Like its twin, this item is decided by a printed audiogram that cannot be re-read here.** The transcription recorded the axes, not the plotted thresholds; **no crop has been cut, so no `image` field is set, and the key is held exactly as printed.**\n\n**What "severe" means to the patient**, which is the reason the band exists *(not taken from the course material — the deck gives the numbers without the functional descriptions)*: at 60–90 dB **no conversational speech is audible at all**. A shout at close range may be heard as sound rather than as words. Amplification is essential and often insufficient on its own, and the audiogram is read alongside speech discrimination to decide whether a hearing aid will help — the deck notes that speech audiometry *"provides an idea about the benefit of a hearing aid"* (`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`).\n\n**Where the boundaries actually bite:**\n\n| Band | Threshold | Practical meaning |\n|---|---|---|\n| Normal | **< 20 dB** | — |\n| Mild | 20–40 dB | soft speech and noisy rooms only |\n| Moderate | 40–60 dB | conversation missed; **hearing aid** — the key of `enthd-ear-143` |\n| **Severe** | **60–90 dB** | **keyed** — conversation inaudible; powerful aid, sometimes implant |\n| Profound | 90–110 dB | **cochlear implant** territory |\n| Total | **> 110 dB** | the deck reserves the word *"deafness"* for *"profound or total hearing loss"* |\n\n**Bottom line: 20, 40, 60, 90 — four numbers, five bands, and the whole family of questions.** The shared table lives in `enthd-ear-143`; this entry points at it rather than repeating it.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A severe hearing loss is a threshold of 60–90 dB, the band in which conversational speech becomes inaudible; profound begins at 90 dB and total above 110 dB.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.54 (Ear ch.6, tested as audiology — filed to `ent-audio`). **The stem depends on a printed pure-tone audiogram; no crop exists yet, so no `image` field is set and the key is held exactly as printed.** The severity bands and the definition of deafness are from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`, the hearing-aid line from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`; the functional descriptions of each band are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. **The shared severity table lives in `enthd-ear-143`; this entry points at it.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-145', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hearing',
  stem:'What is the **earliest manifestation** of the condition shown in the photo attached (**Glomus tumor**)?',
  options:['Pulsatile tinnitus','Bloody otorrhea','Headache','Hearing loss'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Pulsatile tinnitus — the tumour is a mass of blood vessels sitting against the middle ear, so the patient hears their own pulse long before the tumour is big enough to do anything else.**\n\n`L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf` files it under the first of its five types, *"1) From paraauditory structure"*, and names the mechanism and the disease on the same slide: *"**Vascular somatosensation: rhythmic with the heart beat**"*, with **"Paragangliomas"** listed first among the vascular causes, beside *"A-V shunt"* and *"Carotido cavernous fistula"*. Its clinical-evaluation slide adds the character: *"**arterial…. Harsh**"*.\n\n**Why tinnitus comes first, and the other three later:**\n\n| Option | When it appears | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Pulsatile tinnitus** | **first — keyed** | a paraganglioma is intensely vascular and lies **immediately beneath the floor of the middle ear** (glomus jugulare) or **on the promontory** (glomus tympanicum). Turbulent flow inside it is transmitted straight into the ear, so the symptom begins while the tumour is still millimetres across |\n| **Bloody otorrhea** | **late** | it requires the tumour to have **eroded through the drum** and be ulcerating in the canal. `L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf` and `L17,18) Otorrhea and otalgia.pdf` both list *"Bloody Otorrhea — Tumor: **Glomus**, Carcinoma of EAC"*, which is a presentation of established disease |\n| **Headache** | **late or never** | needs intracranial extension. Not a feature of a small middle-ear paraganglioma |\n| **Hearing loss** | **intermediate** | conductive, and only once the mass is large enough to load the drum or the ossicular chain. It follows the tinnitus, it does not precede it |\n\n**⚠️ Two practical points the decks do not print** *(not taken from the course material)*: the tumour is seen as a **red or bluish mass behind an intact drum** which blanches under pneumatic pressure (**Brown’s sign**), and **it must never be biopsied through the drum** — it can bleed torrentially. Diagnosis is by imaging, CT for bone erosion and MRI for soft tissue, with pre-operative embolisation.\n\n**⚠️ Recorded gap in the cache.** `L17,18) Otorrhea and otalgia.pdf` promises a full glomus section — its slide headings read *"Glomus tumors: **Pathology · Main types · Clinical presentation · Investigations · Treatment**"* — but the cached text carries **the headings alone with no content beneath them**, so the detail on that deck is unreadable from the cache. The grounding above is therefore taken from `L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf`, which does print prose.\n\n**Cross-bank agreement, and it is exact.** Grade Gain prints this identical question against its own photograph — *"the earliest manifestation of the tumour shown in the photograph below"*, options **bloody otorrhea / pain / pulsatile tinnitus / hearing loss** — and keys **pulsatile tinnitus** (`entqb-ear7-180`). The endpoint bank asks the association twice and keys glomus both times (`entep-ear-3`, `entep-ear-231`). **Three banks, one answer.**\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A glomus tumour announces itself as pulsatile tinnitus — a vascular sound heard while the tumour is still tiny; bloody otorrhoea, hearing loss and headache are all later.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.55 (Ear ch.7, Tinnitus and hearing loss). Secondary chapter: `ent-otorrhea`, where glomus appears as a cause of bloody discharge. **The stem promises a photograph but names the tumour in the stem itself, so the item is answerable without the figure; no crop exists and no `image` field is set.** The vascular type of tinnitus, its rhythm and character, and bloody otorrhoea from glomus are transcribed from `L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf`, with the bloody-otorrhoea list also in `L17,18) Otorrhea and otalgia.pdf`. **⚠️ Recorded: `L17,18`’s glomus section survives in the cache as headings only, with no text beneath them.** Brown’s sign, the biopsy contraindication and the imaging are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. **Cross-bank agreement with `entqb-ear7-180` (same question, same key, one option changed), `entep-ear-3` and `entep-ear-231`.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-146', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hearing',
  stem:'All of the following are among the **causes of tinnitus accompanying SNHL except**?',
  options:['Presbyacusis','Noise-induced hearing loss','Meniere’s disease','Foreign body impacted in external auditory canal'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**A foreign body in the canal — because that is a CONDUCTIVE block, and the deck gives conductive tinnitus its own separate type.**\n\n`L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf` classifies tinnitus into five types, and the two that matter here are printed one above the other:\n\n> *"**2) Conductive tinnitus: Tinnitus aggreviated by CHL.** **3) SNHL Tinnitus: Presbyacusis. Noise induced hearing loss. acoustic neuroma. Meniere’s disease.**"*\n\n**Three of the four options are on the SNHL list verbatim.** The fourth is not on it, and it is not an oversight — an impacted foreign body is listed by `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` under conductive causes: *"External auditory canal: … 2 – **Complete obstruction: Impacted wax. Foreign body.** Debris or discharge. Tumour."*\n\n| Option | Type of loss | On which list |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Presbyacusis** | **SNHL** | named on the SNHL-tinnitus slide; `L19.1` lists *"Aging: presbyacusis"* under sensorineural causes |\n| **Noise-induced hearing loss** | **SNHL** | named on the same slide; `L19.1`: *"Traumatic: barotrauma, **noise trauma**"* |\n| **Meniere’s disease** | **SNHL** | named on the same slide, and `L19.1` lists *"Meniere’s disease"* among sensorineural causes |\n| **Foreign body in the EAC** | **conductive — keyed as the exception** | it obstructs the canal; the cochlea and nerve are untouched |\n\n**⚠️ Note what the key does NOT say: a foreign body does not cause tinnitus at all.** It does. The deck’s own type 2 exists precisely because a conductive block **aggravates** tinnitus — by removing the ambient masking noise that normally covers it, so the patient becomes aware of a sound that was always there *(the masking mechanism is not stated on the slide and is not taken from the course material)*. **The question is not about whether tinnitus occurs but about which category it belongs to**, and a foreign body belongs to conductive tinnitus, not to SNHL tinnitus.\n\n**The fourth member of the SNHL list is worth carrying too, since it is the dangerous one:** the deck names **acoustic neuroma** alongside the other three. **A unilateral tinnitus with a unilateral sensorineural loss is imaged, not reassured** — Grade Gain keys exactly that in `entqb-ear7-181`.\n\n**Bottom line: tinnitus with SNHL is cochlear or neural — presbyacusis, noise, Ménière’s, schwannoma. Tinnitus with CHL is the old sound heard newly, because the masking has been taken away.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear7-179` (most tinnitus is idiopathic), `entqb-ear7-182`, `entqb-ear7-183`, `entqb-ear7-181`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Presbyacusis, noise-induced loss, Ménière’s disease and acoustic neuroma are the deck’s SNHL-tinnitus list; an impacted foreign body causes conductive tinnitus instead.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.55 (Ear ch.7). The five-type classification, the conductive and SNHL tinnitus lists are transcribed from `L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf`; foreign body under *"complete obstruction"* of the canal, and presbyacusis, noise trauma and Ménière’s among sensorineural causes, from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`. **The masking explanation for conductive tinnitus is added and is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.** Cross-reference verified: `entqb-ear7-181`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-147', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hearing',
  stem:'**Sound pressure transformer system of the middle ear** magnifies sound energy by …. **dB**?',
  options:['10','20','22','26'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**26 dB — and the trap is option C, because 22 is the same fact expressed as a RATIO rather than in decibels.**\n\n`L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` builds the number in three steps and prints both figures in one sentence:\n\n| Step | The deck’s own words |\n|---|---|\n| **The problem** | *"**99.9% of sound energy is lost** when the sound pass from air to a fluid medium"* |\n| **Hydraulic (areal) ratio** | *"Ratio between the surface area of the ear drum (90mm) & that of the foot plate of stapes (3mm). **Vibrating area of the T.M = 55mm. Areal ratio = 55/3 = 17**"* |\n| **Ossicular lever** | *"Ratio between the length of the **handle of malleus** to the **long process of incus** = **1.3**"* |\n| **The product** | *"Sound P transformer mechanism of the middle ear = areal ratio x ossicul. Lever action = **22** (**this increases the sound energy by 26dB**)"* |\n\n**So 17 × 1.3 ≈ 22 is the pressure gain as a plain multiple, and 26 dB is that same gain written on the decibel scale.** The option list offers both numbers side by side, and the stem asks for **dB** — read the unit before choosing.\n\n| Option | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| **10 dB** | not a figure the deck gives; a plausible-sounding decoy |\n| **20 dB** | not the deck’s number either — though it is the boundary of **normal hearing**, *"Normal < 20dB"*, which is why it sits comfortably in the list |\n| **22** | **the ratio, not the decibels** — the deck’s own 17 × 1.3. The single best distractor in the question |\n| **26** | **keyed — the transformer gain in dB** |\n\n**Why the middle ear needs a transformer at all.** Air is light and compressible, cochlear fluid is not, so sound striking a fluid surface directly is almost entirely reflected — the deck’s 99.9 % loss, which is about 30 dB. The middle ear recovers most of that by two mechanical tricks: collecting pressure over the **large** drum and delivering it through the **small** footplate (the 17-fold areal ratio), and the small mechanical advantage of the ossicular lever (1.3).\n\n**And the clinical corollary, which is why the number is examined:** destroy the transformer completely and you lose about that much hearing. **A maximal conductive loss is therefore of the order of 60 dB, not 100** — the sound still reaches the cochlea by bone conduction and by striking the round window directly *(the 60 dB figure is not taken from the course material)*.\n\n**Bottom line: 17 × 1.3 = 22 = 26 dB. The ratio and the decibels are the same fact; the question decides which one it wants by its final word.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear7-185` (ossicular discontinuity, which throws the transformer away), `entqb-ear7-183`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The middle-ear transformer multiplies sound pressure about 22-fold — areal ratio 17 times ossicular lever 1.3 — which is a gain of 26 dB, offsetting the 99.9 % lost at the air-to-fluid interface.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.55 (Ear ch.7). Secondary chapter: `ent-earanat`, where the ossicular chain itself is taught. The 99.9 % loss, the areal ratio 55/3 = 17, the ossicular lever 1.3, the product 22 and *"this increases the sound energy by 26dB"* are all transcribed verbatim from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`, as is *"Normal < 20dB"*. **The maximal-conductive-loss figure of 60 dB is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material.** Cross-reference verified: `entqb-ear7-185`.' }
,

{ id:'enthd-ear-148', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-audio',
  stem:'**Prolonged Schwabach test** indicates which of the following?',
  options:['CHL','SNHL','MHL','Normal hearing'],
  answer:0,
  explanation:'**Conductive hearing loss — the lecture prints the result and its meaning on one line.**\n\n`L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`, *Schwabach Test — "Determines if ear is Conductive or Sensorineural"*, comparing *"Examiner’s BC"* with *"Patient’s BC"*:\n\n| Result | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| **Ex > Pt (Shortened Schwabach)** | **Normal (or SNHL)** |\n| **Pt > Ex (Prolonged Schwabach)** | **Conductive HL** |\n\n**How the test is done, and why "prolonged" means what it means.** The fork is pressed on the patient’s mastoid until they stop hearing it, then moved straight to the examiner’s. If the **patient** heard it longer than the examiner still can, the patient’s bone conduction is *better than normal* — **prolonged**. That is the signature of a conductive block: bone conduction bypasses the diseased canal, drum and ossicles and reaches an intact cochlea, while the block simultaneously shuts out the room’s ambient noise, so the bone-conducted tone is heard unusually clearly and for unusually long. **The same masking effect is why the Weber lateralises to the bad ear in a conductive loss.**\n\n| Option | Schwabach result |\n|---|---|\n| **CHL** | **prolonged — keyed** |\n| **SNHL** | **shortened** — the cochlea is damaged, so bone conduction fails early and the examiner outlasts the patient |\n| **MHL** | a conductive **and** a sensorineural component together. Bone conduction is depressed by the sensorineural half, so the test cannot come out prolonged; it is the least readable of the four |\n| **Normal hearing** | the deck pairs *normal* with **shortened**, alongside SNHL — the examiner, having normal bone conduction, hears the dying fork a moment longer simply because the patient stopped first |\n\n**⚠️ Note the deck’s own pairing, because it is the reason the test is never used alone.** *"Shortened"* covers **both** normal hearing and sensorineural loss, so a shortened Schwabach does not distinguish the two. It is the third leg of the tuning-fork tripod, run with Rinne and Weber, and it earns its place by confirming the conductive result from a third direction: **conductive loss = Rinne negative + Weber to the same ear + Schwabach prolonged.**\n\n**⚠️ A cross-bank discrepancy already on record, and this printing agrees with the slide.** The endpoint bank’s `entep-ear-185` presents a conductive ear with a **normal** Schwabach; Grade Gain’s `entqb-ear8-223` keys **prolonged**, as does this item and as does `L13,14`. Both printings are held as transcribed; **the discrepancy is recorded, not resolved.**\n\nCross-references: `entqb-ear8-223`, `entep-ear-185`.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'A prolonged Schwabach — the patient’s bone conduction outlasting the examiner’s — means conductive hearing loss; shortened covers both normal hearing and sensorineural loss.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.55 (Ear ch.7, but a tuning-fork item — filed to `ent-audio`). The Schwabach table and the Rinne and Weber rules are transcribed from `L13,14) AudioVestibulae Evaluation.pdf`; the technique and the masking explanation are added and are not on the slide. **⚠️ Cross-bank discrepancy recorded, key held: `entep-ear-185` prints a conductive ear with a NORMAL Schwabach, while this item, `entqb-ear8-223` and the slide all say PROLONGED.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-149', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hearing',
  stem:'Which of the following correctly describes the **nature of tinnitus**?',
  options:['Venous tinnitus, harsh','Arterial tinnitus, hums','Venous tinnitus, hums','None of the above'],
  answer:2,
  explanation:'**Venous tinnitus hums; arterial tinnitus is harsh. Options A and B are the correct pair with the adjectives swapped.**\n\n`L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf` prints the two characters under *Clinical evaluations*, in the history section:\n\n> *"**Character; venous…. Huming ; arterial….Harsh**"*\n\nand the same deck names the sources on its types slide: under *"1) From paraauditory structure — Somatosensation"* it lists *"**Vascular somatosensation: rhythmic with the heart beat**"* and *"**Venous Humps**"* — the deck’s spelling of **venous hums** — with **paragangliomas, A-V shunt and carotido-cavernous fistula** as the vascular causes.\n\n**Why the two sound different, which makes the pair easy to keep straight** *(the fluid-dynamic explanation is not on the slide and is not taken from the course material)*: **arterial** flow is **pulsatile** and turbulent, driven by systole against a resistant wall, so it is heard as a rough, **harsh** sound **synchronous with the heartbeat**. **Venous** flow is **continuous and low-pressure**, so it is heard as a soft, steady **hum** — and it typically changes with head position or with light pressure on the internal jugular vein, which is the bedside manoeuvre that identifies it.\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Venous, harsh** | the two halves belong to different vessels — this is the swap |\n| **Arterial, hums** | the other half of the same swap |\n| **Venous, hums** | **keyed** — verbatim from the slide |\n| **None of the above** | unavailable once C matches the deck word for word |\n\n**⚠️ Read what "nature" is asking.** The stem uses the deck’s history heading — under *"Clinical evaluations"* it asks for **site** (*"unilateral……..?"*), **character** and **association** (*"vertigo, SNHL"*). This question is about **character**, and character is a vascular question: the deck’s first type of tinnitus is the para-auditory one, where a **real sound** exists and can sometimes be heard by the examiner. That is why character matters at all — the rest of tinnitus, the conductive and sensorineural types, has no sound to describe.\n\n**Bottom line: harsh and pulsatile = arterial; a steady hum = venous. Both are vascular, both belong to the para-auditory type, and both are worth listening for with a stethoscope over the mastoid and the neck.** Cross-references: `entqb-ear7-182` (vibratory versus non-vibratory, and why only vibratory tinnitus can be objective), `entqb-ear7-179`, `entep-ear-231`, and `enthd-ear-145` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Venous tinnitus is a continuous hum, arterial tinnitus is harsh and pulsatile — the deck’s two-word character rule for para-auditory vascular tinnitus.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.55 (Ear ch.7). *"Character; venous…. Huming ; arterial….Harsh"*, *"Venous Humps"* and the vascular causes list are transcribed from `L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf` (the slide’s own spellings). The fluid-dynamic reason for the two characters and the jugular-compression manoeuvre are added and are tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. Cross-references verified: `entqb-ear7-182`, `entep-ear-231`.' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-150', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-hearing',
  stem:'All of the following **drug groups are among the drugs used in treatment of tinnitus except**?',
  options:['Tranquillizers','Anaesthetics','Anticonvulsants','Antiarrythmics'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Antiarrhythmics — because the deck’s medical treatment slide names three groups and this is not one of them.**\n\n`L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf`, under *Treatment*: *"1) **Treatment of the cause.** 2) **Medical treatment: Anticonvulsant, IV lidocain, tranquillizer, Transtympanic lidocain.** 3) **Tinnitus maskers.**"*\n\n| Option | On the slide? | As what |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Tranquillizers** | **yes** | *"tranquillizer"* — named outright. Tinnitus is distressing out of proportion to its loudness and the deck records that **25 % of sufferers need medical therapy**; anxiolysis breaks the attention-distress loop rather than the sound |\n| **Anaesthetics** | **yes** | **lidocaine, twice** — *"IV lidocain"* and *"Transtympanic lidocain"*. Lidocaine is a **local anaesthetic**, and it is the only drug the deck names by name |\n| **Anticonvulsants** | **yes** | *"Anticonvulsant"* — named outright, on the model of tinnitus as aberrant neural firing that a membrane-stabilising drug can quieten |\n| **Antiarrhythmics** | **NOT on the slide — keyed** | no antiarrhythmic drug or class appears anywhere in the deck’s treatment section |\n\n**⚠️ THE KEY IS DEFENSIBLE ONLY ON THE DECK’S OWN CLASSIFICATION, AND THAT IS WORTH RECORDING RATHER THAN GLOSSING.** **Lidocaine is itself a class Ib antiarrhythmic** — it is a standard drug for ventricular arrhythmia, and it shares one mechanism with its anaesthetic action: **sodium-channel blockade** *(the pharmacological classification is not taken from the course material; the ENT deck names lidocaine without classifying it)*. So a candidate who classified the deck’s own named drug pharmacologically rather than by its ENT label would find that **the excepted group is represented on the list by lidocaine itself**.\n\n**The examiner’s intent is nevertheless clear and the key stands**: the deck presents lidocaine as an **anaesthetic** — it is given *transtympanically*, into the middle ear, which is an anaesthetic route and not an antiarrhythmic one — so *anaesthetics* is a group that is used and *antiarrhythmics* is a group that is not named. **A defective-looking key is noted, never disputed.**\n\n**What the slide is really teaching, and it is the more useful point:** the first line of treatment is *"treatment of the cause"*, and the drug list sits **second**, after that has failed. Nothing on the list abolishes tinnitus; the anticonvulsant and the anaesthetic aim at the neural generator, the tranquilliser at the distress, and the **tinnitus masker** — third on the slide — works by covering the sound rather than removing it. **Tinnitus is managed, not cured**, which is why `L19.2` opens by insisting *"tinnitus is a symptom and is not a disease"*.\n\nCross-references: `entqb-ear7-179` (most tinnitus is idiopathic, so most of it has no cause to treat), `entqb-ear7-181` (unilateral tinnitus is imaged first), `entqb-ear7-182`, and `enthd-ear-146`/`enthd-ear-149` in this batch.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'The deck’s medical treatment of tinnitus is anticonvulsants, lidocaine (intravenous and transtympanic) and tranquillisers, plus maskers — antiarrhythmics are not named, though lidocaine is pharmacologically one.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.56 (Ear ch.7). The whole treatment slide, *"tinnitus is a symptom and is not a disease"* and the 25 %-need-therapy figure are transcribed from `L19.2) Tinnitus.pdf` (which spells *"lidocain"*). **⚠️ ARGUABLE KEY RECORDED, KEY HELD: lidocaine — the only drug the deck names, and the basis of the "anaesthetics" option — is itself a class Ib ANTIARRHYTHMIC, so the excepted group is arguably present on the list. The bank classifies it as an anaesthetic and the printed key is kept.** The pharmacological classification is tagged in the explanation as outside the course material. **No other bank prints a tinnitus-treatment question — checked across `app/data/questions.ent.js`.**' },

{ id:'enthd-ear-151', bank:'house', module:'ent', chapter:'ent-aom',
  stem:'What is the **most common cause of hearing loss in children**?',
  options:['Impacted wax','Post-meningitis','Presbyacusis','Secretory otitis media'],
  answer:3,
  explanation:'**Secretory otitis media — glue ear. It is a disease of exactly this age group, it is painless, and its cardinal complaint is deafness.**\n\n`L21.2) Otitis media with effusion.pdf` defines it as *"a **non suppurative middle ear fluid**"* and pins the epidemiology in three lines: *"**It is the disease of infants and young age. Higher incidence from 6-24 month. Increases in winter and spring.**"* Its first listed clinical problem is *"**1) Decrease hearing**"*.\n\n**Why children and not adults.** The same deck gives the mechanism: *"E.T. ventilates the middle ear and **cleare fluid** from the middle ear to the nasopharynx… so when closed; gases from the middle ear is **absorbed** → increase the negative intratympanic pressure; **midlle ear transudation**"*, and lists **adenoids** among the *"related clinical conditions"*. A child’s Eustachian tube is short, wide and horizontal and its nasopharyngeal end is surrounded by adenoid, so every cold blocks it *(the tube’s childhood dimensions are not on this deck and are not taken from the course material)*. `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf` files the result under conductive causes: *"Middle ear fluid: - **Serous: S.O.M.**"*, with *"Eustachian tube dysfun."* beside it.\n\n| Option | Why it is not the answer |\n|---|---|\n| **Impacted wax** | a real and common childhood finding, and the commonest cause of conductive loss **in adults** (`entqb-ear7-184`). But wax causes **no hearing loss until the canal is completely occluded**, so most impactions are silent; effusion outnumbers it as a cause of actual deafness |\n| **Post-meningitis** | the classic cause of **acquired profound sensorineural deafness** in a child — bacterial meningitis with cochlear ossification, and the reason such children are referred urgently for implantation *(not taken from the course material — no cached ENT deck names meningitis; `L19.1` reaches it only as *"Inflam.: bacterial, viral or specific"* among sensorineural causes)*. Devastating, but **rare**. **Severity is not frequency** |\n| **Presbyacusis** | **impossible in a child by definition** — `L19.1` lists it as *"Aging: presbyacusis"*. It is the commonest sensorineural loss of the **elderly** (`entqb-ear7-186`, `entqb-ear7-191`) and is in this option list purely as an age trap |\n| **Secretory otitis media** | **keyed** |\n\n**⚠️ Why it matters more than the mild loss suggests.** The effusion is painless and afebrile, so it is missed — the child is called inattentive, turns the television up, or is thought slow. The loss is only **20–40 dB**, but it falls in the years in which **speech and language are acquired**, which is why persistent bilateral glue ear is treated with grommets rather than watched indefinitely.\n\n**Cross-bank agreement:** Grade Gain asks the same question restricted to **conductive** loss and keys *"secretory otitis media due to Eustachian tube dysfunction"* (`entqb-ear7-190`); the same bank’s adult mirror keys impacted wax (`entqb-ear7-184`), and its childhood **sensorineural** mirror keys hereditary causes (`entqb-ear7-189`). **This item drops the qualifier and asks for hearing loss outright — the answer does not change, because effusion is common enough to dominate the whole category.**\n\n**⚠️ And the adult mirror, worth carrying:** the same effusion, **unilateral, in an adult**, is nasopharyngeal carcinoma until the nasopharynx has been examined.\n\nWritten for this bank — d house ENT mcq prints no explanation here.',
  objective:'Secretory otitis media is the commonest cause of hearing loss in children — painless, bilateral, 20–40 dB, at the age when speech is being learned; presbyacusis is impossible and post-meningitic deafness is severe but rare.',
  source:'Dr. HOUSE in ENT, End-of-round MCQs 2025, p.56 (Ear ch.7, but the disease is otitis media with effusion — filed to `ent-aom`; secondary chapter `ent-hearing`). The definition, *"the disease of infants and young age"*, *"higher incidence from 6-24 month"*, the Eustachian tube mechanism, adenoids and *"decrease hearing"* are transcribed from `L21.2) Otitis media with effusion.pdf`; *"Middle ear fluid — Serous: S.O.M."*, *"Aging: presbyacusis"* and the sensorineural inflammatory line from `L19.1) hearing loss.pdf`. **Post-meningitic deafness and the childhood dimensions of the Eustachian tube are supplied from general knowledge and tagged in the explanation — no cached ENT deck names meningitis as a cause of deafness.** **Cross-bank agreement with `entqb-ear7-190`**, which asks the same question restricted to conductive loss.' }
